A Window into Havana with @cubareporter

For more scenes from Cuba, be sure to follow @cubareporter as well as @khaversiddiqi and @low843 who are also in the country.
CNN International correspondent Patrick Oppmann (@cubareporter) has lived in Cuba for over two years with his wife and two children. Patrick shares scenes of daily life in Havana on Instagram, opening up a window into a part of the world many long to see and visit. &ldquo;While the government lifted a ban on cell phones in 2008, Cubans do not have access to 3G networks and smart phones are an impossible expense in a country where most people make $20 a month. And, because of the US travel ban, Cuba is still off-limits to most Americans,&rdquo; Patrick explains. &ldquo;I feel particularly fortunate that I have been able to see so much of the island and report from here during a time when Cuba is undergoing long-awaited economic reforms.&rdquo;
Patrick&rsquo;s photos and videos document a city in the midst of change. &ldquo;Havana is a strikingly beautiful city that has seen better days. Due to neglect and the punishing climate, much of the city has the appearance of a sunken ship. Architects estimate that every day several historic buildings collapse, so there is a sense of racing against time to photograph places that will soon disappear.&rdquo; One of Patrick&rsquo;s favorite places to photograph is the Malec&oacute;n seawall that protects the city. &ldquo;It has been called the largest sofa in the world and each day thousands of Cubans take to the wall to take a seat, share a bottle of rum, play music or just cool off from the stifling heat.&rdquo; He also loves shooting street scenes: &ldquo;Havana is a very spontaneous city and I always try and have a camera at the ready. Some of my favorite photographs are of things I happened to catch out of the corner of my eye; a guy riding on a cart being pulled by a goat, a teenager with an anti-government slogan tattooed on his neck, a fading billboard celebrating the revolution or two boys practicing their boxing stances in the street.&rdquo;
Patrick&rsquo;s inspiration comes from the the responses to his photos and videos. &ldquo;I read lots of comments from Americans who wish they could visit the incredible island that&rsquo;s just 90 miles away. I also enjoy receiving comments from many Cubans who, because of politics or Cuba&rsquo;s financial problems, have had to leave the island,&rdquo; he says. &ldquo;They have written me to say that my photos provide a window to the homeland that they desperately miss. To have that kind of impact is both an extraordinary opportunity and privilege.&rdquo;
